# What is carried here and what is not turns on reproducibility, not on size.
#
# The simulation CSVs are carried despite being large. They are deterministic —
# `run_sim.py` and `diagnose_c3.py` produce byte-identical files — but that is a
# claim about this machine, and the point of carrying them is that someone can
# recompute every number in the write-up without owning a machine that reproduces
# our bytes. They are the only place `sim/` and `analysis/` meet.
#
# What follows is ignored because rerunning is not the issue: none of it is
# reproducible at all. A capture is of a network that no longer exists in that
# state, and raw tool output is an artifact of one run. Those are published as a
# release artifact, referenced by run id from `runs.ndjson`.
raw/
captures/

# Dry-run output is neither evidence nor irreplaceable: it is an apparatus check,
# regenerated by `run_block.py --dry-run` in seconds, and its records are stubbed.
dryrun.ndjson
dryrun-raw/
